  ],
  "text": "Voyager one launched in nineteen seventy seven and still sends us a signal from beyond the solar system. It is over twenty four billion kilometres out. The signal takes about twenty two hours to arrive. It launched because of a planetary alignment that comes round roughly every hundred and seventy five years. Line the outer planets up right and one probe can visit several without carrying fuel for any of it. Because it does not simply fly past a planet. It steals from it. Swing close and the planet's motion flings you onward, and the planet slows immeasurably. Its power comes from decaying plutonium, and that output has been falling since launch. Instruments have been switched off one at a time, to keep the transmitter alive. It carries a gold record of sounds and pictures, addressed to nobody in particular. And it turned around once to photograph us. The whole planet was less than a single pixel. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
